id: ops-uizs
title: Reconciliation proves an artifact is attested but never that it came from the commit about to be tagged
description: "The release step treats a publish as already done when the version exists on the registry and its dist.attestations field is non-empty. Neither read says which source commit produced that artifact. The consequence is not hypothetical: this fleet has repeatedly published a version and then failed to push its tag, because the tag push raced branch protection. A later run computing the same date-derived version finds the coordinate occupied and attested, reconciles, and tags its own HEAD. The git tag and the GitHub release then describe source that the immutable tarball on the registry does not contain, and the release notes describe changes that are not in the published artifact. The attestation makes this worse rather than better, because a bundle produced by the earlier commit satisfies a check that only asks whether one exists. Reconciliation must compare identity, not presence: resolve the attestation bundle for the exact version and require its recorded source commit to equal the commit being tagged, refusing when they differ instead of tagging around the mismatch. Raised independently by Greptile as two P1 security findings on the attestation wave, on pm-csv PR 87."
type: Issue
status: open
priority: 1
tags[3]: "area:release","area:supply-chain","type:defect"
created_at: "2026-08-28T14:48:16.014Z"
updated_at: "2026-08-28T15:07:22.963Z"
author: claude
dependencies[1]{id,kind,created_at,author,source_kind,author_source}:
