comments[1]{created_at,author,text}:
  "2026-08-28T15:07:22.963Z",claude,"CodeRabbit reached the same conclusion independently on pm-brief and researched the mechanism, which saves the design step. There is no single npm command that verifies the binding. The registry exposes the signed bundle at GET https://registry.npmjs.org/-/npm/v1/attestations/NAME@VERSION, which returns a Sigstore bundle carrying an in-toto statement. Three fields in it answer the question this gate needs: the subject digest, which must match the tarball the registry is serving; the build definition's repository, which must be this repository; and the recorded source commit, which must equal the commit about to be tagged. npm audit signatures is not a substitute, because it checks registry signatures rather than the provenance predicate. The refusal must therefore name which of the three mismatched, so an operator can tell an unrelated artifact from a stale one."
docs[1]{path,scope,note}:
  .github/workflows/release.yml,project,reconciled_attested and refuse_unattested_or_fail
body: ""
